---
// src/components/ComputerIslandSection.astro
//
// Same family as ArtIslandSection.astro: two flanking illustrations
// sitting behind centered-ish text, one JS-driven mouse-repel script,
// one CSS bob loop per island. Layout differs though — per the
// reference screenshot, neither island bleeds off the section's edges
// here (both sit fully inside the frame), and the arrangement is
// left-middle / top-right rather than top-left / bottom-right:
//
//   - `leftImage`  (telephone-pole island, svg)  -> mid-left, roughly
//     vertically centered
//   - `rightImage` (computer island, gif)        -> upper-right,
//     larger
//
// Because nothing bleeds off an edge here, offsets are plain vw/vh
// instead of the clamp(mobileValue, calc(a + b*vw), desktopValue)
// trick used in ArtIslandSection. That trick existed there to bridge
// a fixed *rem* offset (which doesn't scale with viewport width) up to
// a *vw*-scaled width. Since every offset below is already vw/vh, it
// scales continuously with the viewport on its own — no separate
// mobile breakpoint needed for position. If it still looks cramped on
// very narrow phones (the two width clamps have a px floor, so an
// island can stop shrinking before its offset does), add a
// `max-width: 640px` query the same way CornerIsland.astro does, or
// just ask to have it tuned.
//
// NOTE on rightImage: the gif you're using is ~19MB, which is a lot
// for a background decoration — worth converting to a compressed
// .webm/.mp4 (via <video autoplay loop muted playsinline>) or a
// lighter-weight animated webp if you get a chance, since a 19MB file
// will noticeably slow down first load, especially on mobile. Left it
// as a plain <img> with loading="lazy" for now so it doesn't block
// anything above it, but that doesn't shrink the download itself.
//
// Background: originally a real image (`backgroundImage` prop), via
// `background-image` + `background-size: cover`, `min-height: 100vh`
// — same technique every other section in this family uses. The
// goal, per your clarification: every section keeps a CONSISTENT
// height across every screen size, with `cover` cropping whichever
// dimension needs it least to make that happen with no gaps. Briefly
// tried sizing by height only (`auto 100%`, cropping only the sides)
// per your request to stop shortening backgrounds vertically on wide
// screens, but that left visible white space on screens wider (in
// aspect ratio) than the image itself, which you didn't want either —
// back to `cover`.
//
// New: swapped that background image for a plain CSS gradient
// (`linear-gradient`, see `.computer-section` below), matching a
// reference photo you sent — a deep-blue-to-cyan vertical sky gradient.
// The `backgroundImage` prop is gone entirely (removed from `Props`,
// the destructuring, the `<section>`'s inline style, and this file's
// own `Usage` example below) rather than left unused, since nothing
// in this section needs an image file any more. This also retires the
// long-standing "I still don't have the actual background image file"
// caveat that used to sit lower in this comment — there's no image to
// not-have any more.
//
// One more layer on top of that: on a short/wide viewport, `cover`
// still has to crop SOMETHING to avoid a gap, and per your follow-up
// that "something" shouldn't ever be the image's own height either —
// you'd rather scroll than see it shortened. Fixed by adding
// `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` (the image's real ratio) alongside
// `min-height`/`width: 100%` on `.computer-section` below, so the
// section itself grows taller than one screen on those viewports
// instead of asking `cover` to crop into the height — see the comment
// there.
//
// No seam-fade classes any more — the shared fade-band system that
// used to blend section edges together was removed per your request,
// so this section now just butts directly against ArtIslandSection
// above it and EducationIslandSection below it.
//
// Still carries `.sky-chain`, so the gap-collapsing on both sides
// keeps working regardless of what backs it.
//
// Now also carries `.halftone-hover`, joining the single, page-wide
// cursor-spotlight element in Layout.astro (`.halftone-spotlight`,
// see the comment there) purely by having this class — no markup or
// script needed here for it, since `body:has(.halftone-hover:hover)
// .halftone-spotlight` is what actually turns the spotlight on.
//
// New: this section's background/clouds layers (`.computer-gradient-
// bg`, `.computer-clouds-bg`, both explicit `z-index: -1`) exist so
// ArtIslandSection's moon — which deliberately bleeds down past its
// own section's bottom edge into this one — can paint over them
// instead of them winning a stacking tie and covering it. That
// negative z-index USED to live on `.computer-section` itself, but
// that trapped this section's own foreground content (the islands,
// the text) in the same local stacking context, so they were also
// losing to Art's moon without anyone asking for that — invisible
// until the mobile layout moved the right-hand computer island up
// into the overlap zone, which is the "island behind the moon" bug
// you reported. Fixed by moving the negative z-index down onto just
// the background/clouds layers instead of the whole section — see
// the comment on `.computer-section` itself for the full reasoning,
// and ArtIslandSection.astro's own top-of-file comment for the
// moon-bleed side of this.
//
// Usage:
//   <ComputerIslandSection
//     id="section-cs"
//     eyebrow="Island 02"
//     title="Lorem Ipsum Dolor"
//     text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet..."
//     leftImage="/images/telephone_pole_island.svg"
//     rightImage="/images/computer_island.gif"
//     cloudsBackground="/images/computer_island_background_ver2.webp"
//   />

// New: `leftImage`/`cloudsBackground` are imported src/images/
// modules (`ImageMetadata`) rendered via <Image /> — see
// src/pages/index.astro's frontmatter note for why. `rightImage`
// (computer_island.gif) is the one exception in the whole project:
// Astro's built-in image service can't process animated GIFs (it
// throws an "unsupported image format" build error), so per Astro's
// own documented workaround it's still imported as a module — so it's
// in the build via src/, hashed like everything else — but rendered
// with a plain <img>, reading `.src`/`.width`/`.height` off the
// import directly instead of going through <Image />.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import type { ImageMetadata } from 'astro:assets';

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  leftImage: ImageMetadata;
  rightImage: ImageMetadata;
  cloudsBackground: ImageMetadata;
}

const { id, eyebrow, title, text, leftImage, rightImage, cloudsBackground } = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="computer-section sky-chain halftone-hover computer-spotlight-front"
  id={id}
>
  <div class="computer-gradient-bg" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="computer-clouds-bg" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="computer-clouds-track">
      <Image class="computer-clouds-tile" src={cloudsBackground} alt="" />
      <Image class="computer-clouds-tile" src={cloudsBackground} alt="" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="computer-island computer-island-left" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="computer-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <Image src={leftImage} alt="" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    {/* set:html here because this text is a real HTML bullet list
       (see src/i18n/ui.ts) -- <div>, not <p>, since a <ul> can't nest
       inside a <p>. */}
    <div class="body-text" set:html={text} />
  </div>

  <div class="computer-island computer-island-right" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="computer-island-inner parallax-inner">
      <img src={rightImage.src} width={rightImage.width} height={rightImage.height} alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-repel drift as ArtIslandSection.astro, driving every
  // `.parallax-inner` element on the page. If both components end up
  // on the same page, each ships its own copy of this script and each
  // one queries ALL `.parallax-inner` elements globally (not just its
  // own) — that's intentional/harmless (both compute the same easing
  // toward the same targets), just a little redundant. Worth hoisting
  // into one shared script later if a third section joins this family.
  //
  // Wrapped in `astro:page-load` (fires on the initial load AND after
  // every <ClientRouter /> navigation -- see src/layouts/Layout.astro)
  // instead of running at the top level: a language switch swaps in a
  // freshly-parsed page, and this module itself only executes once, so
  // without re-running this init on every navigation the drift would
  // go dead after the first language switch. `cleanupParallax` tears
  // down the previous navigation's rAF loop and `mousemove` listener
  // first, so switching languages back and forth all session never
  // piles up duplicate ones running at once (`window` is never
  // recreated by a soft navigation).
  let cleanupParallax: (() => void) | null = null;

  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => {
    cleanupParallax?.();

    const controller = new AbortController();
    let rafId: number | null = null;

    const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
    const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
      "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
    ).matches;

    if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
      const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
      const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
      const ease = 0.08;

      let mouseX = 0;
      let mouseY = 0;
      let hasMouse = false;

      const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
        el,
        currentX: 0,
        currentY: 0,
      }));

      window.addEventListener(
        "mousemove",
        (e) => {
          mouseX = e.clientX;
          mouseY = e.clientY;
          hasMouse = true;
        },
        { signal: controller.signal }
      );

      function tick() {
        for (const s of state) {
          let targetX = 0;
          let targetY = 0;

          if (hasMouse) {
            const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
            const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
            const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

            const dx = restX - mouseX;
            const dy = restY - mouseY;
            const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

            if (dist < influenceRadius) {
              const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
              const nx = dx / dist;
              const ny = dy / dist;
              targetX = nx * maxOffset * falloff;
              targetY = ny * maxOffset * falloff;
            }
          }

          s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
          s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
          s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
        }
        rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      }

      rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    cleanupParallax = () => {
      controller.abort();
      if (rafId !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(rafId);
    };
  });

  // New: black backdrop + white text on `.content` while this
  // section is the one centered in view, per your request -- see the
  // `.content.is-highlighted` CSS rule for the visual side of this.
  // Kept as its own independent `astro:page-load` listener (this
  // script's top-level code only runs once ever, so re-running the
  // init on every soft navigation is what keeps it alive after a
  // language switch) rather than folded into another listener in
  // this file, so its cleanup/re-init doesn't depend on anything
  // else here.
  let cleanupHighlight: (() => void) | null = null;

  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => {
    cleanupHighlight?.();

    const section = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".computer-section");
    const content = section?.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".content");

    if (section && content) {
      // `rootMargin: "-40% 0px -40% 0px"` shrinks the observer's own
      // trigger area to a thin band across the viewport's vertical
      // MIDDLE, instead of the default "anywhere in the viewport" --
      // with `threshold: 0`, `isIntersecting` then flips true only
      // while this section's box overlaps that middle band, i.e.
      // roughly when the section is centered on screen. A plain
      // `threshold` (percent of the section itself visible) would
      // behave very differently section to section since these vary
      // a lot in height; this approach doesn't depend on that.
      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
        (entries) => {
          for (const entry of entries) {
            content.classList.toggle("is-highlighted", entry.isIntersecting);
          }
        },
        { rootMargin: "-40% 0px -40% 0px", threshold: 0 }
      );

      observer.observe(section);
      cleanupHighlight = () => observer.disconnect();
    }
  });
</script>

<style>
  .computer-section {
    position: relative;
    /* New: no explicit `z-index` here any more (used to be `-1` —
       see the git-blame-shaped history in this comment if you're
       reading an old copy). That value was needed back when this
       section's own full-bleed background image sat directly on
       `.computer-section` itself: giving the SECTION an explicit
       z-index made it establish its own local stacking context,
       which correctly kept that background from covering
       ArtIslandSection's bleeding moon (`0 > -1`, unambiguous) — but
       it had a side effect nobody asked for and didn't show up until
       you tried the site on mobile: EVERY descendant of this section,
       including the two foreground islands (`z-index: 1` — see
       `.computer-island` below) and the text (`z-index: 2`), got
       trapped inside that same local stacking context too. A trapped
       descendant can't out-rank anything outside its ancestor's own
       context no matter how high its own z-index is set — the WHOLE
       `.computer-section` subtree is compared to `.art-section` as
       one flat unit, at `.computer-section`'s own z-index (-1) versus
       `.art-section`'s (0). So the computer island — despite its own
       `z-index: 1` — was ALWAYS painting behind Art's moon in their
       overlap zone, not just the background; it just wasn't visible
       until the mobile layout (`top: 4vh` below, versus 24vh on
       desktop) moved the right-hand computer island up into that
       overlap zone, which is exactly the "island behind the moon"
       bug you reported.

       Fix: stop trying to solve this at the SECTION level. This
       element no longer sets its own z-index, so it no longer
       establishes a stacking context — every descendant below now
       competes directly against `.art-section`'s unit (z-index: 0)
       instead of being trapped as a group first. The two elements
       that actually need to LOSE that comparison (the gradient and
       the clouds — see `.computer-gradient-bg` and `.computer-clouds-
       bg` below, both now `z-index: -1` themselves) carry their OWN
       explicit negative z-index directly, so they alone stay behind
       Art's moon, exactly as before. Everything that should WIN
       (the islands at `z-index: 1`, the text at `z-index: 2`) is left
       untouched and, no longer trapped, now correctly out-ranks Art's
       moon on its own merits (`1 > 0`, `2 > 0`) — fixing the reported
       bug on every viewport, not just mobile, since the same trapping
       applied everywhere, it just wasn't visible until something sat
       up near the top of this section on the overlap zone. */
    /* Bumped up from 100vh to 130vh, then to 150vh, per your two
       earlier requests to make this section longer — the first
       matched the fix already used on BonusIslandSection.astro's
       `.bonus-section` for the same ask; the second was purely to
       give the section more breathing room once the right-hand island
       became correctly visible again, so it didn't feel cramped right
       up against the top edge. New: 150vh is now the MOBILE value
       only (below 641px) — per your follow-up that it should be a
       bit shorter on big screens, there's now a `min-width: 641px`
       override right after the mobile media query below that brings
       it back down to 130vh there, splitting what used to be one
       value in two: mobile keeps the extra room (especially since
       `.content` just got pushed further down there too, and needs
       the space), desktop doesn't carry height it didn't need. Note
       `top: 4vh` / `24vh` below are `vh` units, not percentages of
       this section's own box, so neither of these min-height values
       relocates the two islands relative to the section's own top
       edge — that's a separate, purely literal "make the section
       longer/shorter" change; adjust either value directly if you
       want more/less on either breakpoint. */
    min-height: 150vh;
    /* `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` used to matter for matching the old
       background image's own real dimensions (3840x2160) so `cover`
       never had to crop it top/bottom — with the image gone in favor
       of a gradient (see the top-of-file comment), there's nothing
       left to protect from cropping, but this section's overall SHAPE
       (how tall it grows on unusual viewports) is a separate concern
       from what paints inside it, and changing it wasn't asked for,
       so it's left in place as a plain fixed target ratio: combined
       with `width: 100%` and `min-height`, the browser uses whichever
       constraint needs MORE height — on a viewport wider-than-16:9
       (mainly short/wide windows), the aspect-ratio wins and this
       section grows taller than whichever `min-height` applies at
       that width; on a taller-than-16:9 viewport (e.g. mobile
       portrait), `min-height` wins instead, so the section stays
       exactly at that value. `width: 100%` is required alongside
       `aspect-ratio` — without it, on the min-height-wins branch the
       browser would stretch this section's WIDTH to satisfy the
       ratio instead of its height, overflowing past the viewport
       edges. */
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `overflow: hidden` here — see the comment on `overflow-x:
       hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal
       clipping page-wide, so an island that needs more vertical room
       than this section provides isn't hard-clipped. */
  }

  /* New: the gradient background moved off `.computer-section` itself
     and onto its own dedicated, absolutely-positioned layer — see the
     comment on `.computer-section` above for why (in short: so this
     layer alone can carry the explicit negative z-index needed to
     stay behind ArtIslandSection's bleeding moon, without trapping
     the section's foreground content along with it). Colors are
     unchanged — still sampled from the reference photo you sent
     (top-to-bottom bands at 0/15/30/45/60/75/90/100% height): a deep
     blue at the top easing into a bright cyan that's mostly settled
     in by the 60-70% mark and stays flat the rest of the way down,
     which is why the stops below cluster earlier (0/25/50%) and
     spread out later (75/100%) rather than sitting at even intervals.
     Plain gradients don't need `cover`/`position`/`repeat` (nothing
     to crop or tile). */
  .computer-gradient-bg {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      #2d50ea 0%,
      #2e76ed 25%,
      #CFD7FC 50%,
      #38d5fa 75%,
      #2ddcfc 100%
    );
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Looping clouds layer, sitting on top of the gradient. Back to
     `z-index: 1` (see the history below this element's own past — it
     briefly went to `0` and back). `1` is what makes this layer win
     its comparison against ArtIslandSection's whole unit (that
     section's own explicit `z-index: 0` — see its comment), since
     `.computer-section` doesn't trap its descendants in their own
     stacking context (see its comment above): the part of Art's moon
     leaking down into this section needs to stay tucked behind the
     clouds instead of in front of them, and `1 > 0` decides that
     outright rather than by tiebreak.
     
     A brief detour: this was tried at `z-index: 0` (tied with the
     moon's unit) so that Layout.astro's page-wide cursor-spotlight
     (`.halftone-spotlight`, also `z-index: 0`) would win ITS OWN tie
     against these clouds and paint on top of them — per your report
     that the spotlight wasn't visible over the clouds, and per the
     CSS stacking spec, tied elements paint in DOM/tree order (later
     wins), which the moon → clouds → spotlight document order does
     satisfy on paper. In practice, though, that traded one visible
     problem for another: the moon/clouds seam here isn't a single
     static comparison, it's two independently-ANIMATED layers (the
     moon drifts, these clouds drift too, both via `translateX` loops)
     each getting repainted every frame, and a browser's compositor
     doesn't guarantee a tied stack-level's DOM-order tiebreak stays
     perfectly stable across repaints the way an unambiguous numeric
     win does — which is what you saw as "the transition ... is
     rough". Reverted back to the clean, unambiguous `1 > 0` win here,
     and moved the spotlight-visibility fix to Layout.astro instead:
     see `.computer-spotlight-front` there for how the spotlight now
     gets a scoped, hover-only bump above these clouds specifically,
     without this element's own z-index (or the moon relationship)
     needing to change at all.
     
     `.computer-gradient-bg` right above this is UNCHANGED at
     `z-index: -1` — the gradient itself still stays behind the moon's
     leak (`-1 < 0`), only the clouds sit in front of it, so the
     stacking order through the overlap zone is gradient (back) →
     moon's fading leak → clouds (front) — same three-layer sandwich
     as before either detour. `1` also ties with the foreground
     islands (`z-index: 1` below) rather than staying strictly under
     them, but that tie still resolves correctly by DOM order (both
     islands come AFTER this element in the markup, so they still
     paint on top of it) — see `.computer-island` below; unlike the
     moon/clouds tie above, nothing here is independently animated
     frame-to-frame in a way that would make that particular tiebreak
     flicker, which is why it was safe to leave as-is even while
     chasing the other one down. Still behind the text (`z-index: 2`),
     no tie there. Same two-tile `translateX` loop as ArtIslandSection's
     `.art-clouds-bg` / InBetweenSection2's `.in-between-2-clouds-bg`
     — see either file's top-of-file comment for the full mechanics
     (real `<img>` tiles sized by `height: 100%; width: auto` rather
     than `background-image` + `cover`, animated `transform:
     translateX` from -50% to 0% on a `width: max-content` track).
     `computer_island_background_ver2.webp` (3840x2160, RGBA) had the
     same kind of seam problem `in_between_2_clouds_background.webp`
     needed fixing for earlier — its own left and right edges didn't
     match (measured ~27.5 average, ~249 max per-channel difference)
     — so it went through the identical roll-to-center + mirrored-
     cross-fade reprocessing before being wired up here; the edge
     mismatch is now ~0.27 average, ~0.8 max. `overflow: hidden` clips
     the track (wider than the section, holding two full tiles) down
     to the section's own bounds. */
  .computer-clouds-bg {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .computer-clouds-track {
    display: flex;
    width: max-content;
    height: 100%;
    /* Back to animated drift. Briefly set to `none` right after this
       first went in, on a request to stop "the background gradient"
       from moving — turned out that was about this clouds layer, not
       the CSS gradient underneath (which was never animated to begin
       with, so it needed no change either time); you clarified you
       do want `computer_island_background_ver2.webp` itself drifting,
       so this is back to the same two-tile `translateX` loop as
       ArtIslandSection's `.art-clouds-bg` / InBetweenSection2's
       `.in-between-2-clouds-bg`. */
    animation: computer-clouds-drift 90s linear infinite;
  }

  .computer-clouds-tile {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    width: auto;
    max-width: none;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    /* New: explicit `aspect-ratio` (cloudsBackground's own real size)
       — <Image /> defaults to `loading="lazy"`, and Chrome flags a
       lazy image whose box isn't pinned by fixed width+height or a
       declared `aspect-ratio`; `height: 100%; width: auto;` alone
       doesn't count, so this states the real ratio directly. */
    aspect-ratio: 3840 / 2160;
  }

  @keyframes computer-clouds-drift {
    from {
      transform: translateX(-50%);
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(0%);
    }
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;

    text-align: left;
  }

  /* New: instead of the whole `.content` box flashing to black as
     one unit, each of the three text pieces below (eyebrow, title,
     body) now gets its OWN black backdrop that sweeps in from the
     left, like a highlighter pen, and they fire one after another --
     eyebrow first, then the title, then the body text -- while this
     section is the one centered in the viewport. `.is-highlighted`
     is still just one class, toggled by the same IntersectionObserver
     as before; the staggering and the sweep itself are done entirely
     with `transition-delay` and an animated `clip-path` on each
     element's own `::before`, so no JS changes were needed here.
     Each text element got `position: relative; isolation: isolate;`
     so its `::before` (with `z-index: -1`) stays confined to that
     element's own local stacking context instead of the page's. */
  .content.is-highlighted .eyebrow {
    color: #ccc;
    transition-delay: 0s;
  }

  .content.is-highlighted .eyebrow::before {
    clip-path: inset(0 0% 0 0);
    transition-delay: 0s;
  }

  .content.is-highlighted h2 {
    color: #fff;
    transition-delay: 0.15s;
  }

  .content.is-highlighted h2::before {
    clip-path: inset(0 0% 0 0);
    transition-delay: 0.15s;
  }

  .content.is-highlighted .body-text {
    color: #eee;
    transition-delay: 0.3s;
  }

  .content.is-highlighted .body-text::before {
    clip-path: inset(0 0% 0 0);
    transition-delay: 0.3s;
  }

  .content.is-highlighted .body-text :global(a) {
    color: #fff;
    text-decoration-color: #aaa;
    transition-delay: 0.3s;
  }


